Bootstrap: localimage
From: /mnt/home_lustre/sedlam56/projects/REALM/realm_og391.sif

# Derived image: the existing SIF plus the one OmniGibson patch the vectorized eval path needs.
#
# WHY A DERIVED BUILD RATHER THAN A REBUILD. realm_og391.def bootstraps from
# docker://stanfordvl/behavior:3.9.1 and then does the whole dm-robotics / openpi-client pip
# install, which means re-pulling a large base and redoing every install to change one file.
# Bootstrapping from the existing 13 GB SIF applies the patch in seconds. The canonical recipe is
# realm_og391.def, which now carries the same patch and the same build guard -- this file exists so
# the change can be TESTED before anyone pays for a clean rebuild, and should be deleted once
# realm_og391.sif has been rebuilt from the canonical def.
#
# WHAT THIS BUYS. Without the patch, running the vector env against the image's own OmniGibson
# (i.e. without binding the OG-lite fork over it) is broken: scene-file objects load ~100 m too high
# in every scene except index 0, so the task object has nothing to rest on. Measured 2026-08-14 at
# num_envs=2, both Default and V-SC:
#
#     scene 0: every check passes
#     scene 1: "main object left the table (z=0.015)", distractor displaced 3.51 m
#
# and that ONE cause is the only thing that failed -- no crash, no contact-view damage, no
# init-queue failure. Scene 0's origin IS the world origin, which is why no single-env run and no
# historical REALM result ever showed it.
#
# The rest of the OG-lite delta (7 files, ~570 lines: incremental contact cache, proximity gate,
# descriptor-pool raise, render-on-demand plumbing) is performance, not correctness, at these scene
# counts -- visible as 268 contact rows here vs 49-51 with the fork. Deliberately NOT included:
# the point of this image is to find out whether the perturbations work on stock OmniGibson with the
# minimum possible change.

%files
    realm/misc/scene_base_zoffset_og391.patch /opt/scene_base_zoffset_og391.patch

%post
    set -e
    patch -p1 -d /behavior-src/OmniGibson < /opt/scene_base_zoffset_og391.patch
    rm /opt/scene_base_zoffset_og391.patch

    # Fail the build loudly rather than shipping an image that silently lacks the fix -- the failure
    # it prevents is a plausible-looking run in which one scene's objects are on the floor.
    grep -q "Re-apply the object poses now that the scene prim is at its final position" \
        /behavior-src/OmniGibson/omnigibson/scenes/scene_base.py
    echo "z-offset patch present"
